## 4. Main Functional Modules

### Module A — Project Repository
Store structured information about every project.

Candidate information:
- Project ID
- Project title
- Description
- Academic year
- Project type (EDI/AACP/etc.)
- Department
- Domain
- Status
- Faculty mentor
- Team members
- Technologies
- Tags
- Optional project outcome/details

### Module B — Project Search & Discovery
This is the primary student-facing feature.

Users should be able to search by:
- Keyword
- Tag
- Domain
- Technology
- Project type
- Academic year
- Department

The search must be implemented through relational database queries.

### Module C — Tag-Based Discovery
Projects and tags should have a many-to-many relationship.

Example:

Power Theft Detection
→ theft
→ electricity
→ energy
→ IoT
→ security

CCTV Theft Detection
→ theft
→ CCTV
→ camera
→ security

Searching for `theft` can therefore retrieve both projects even when the exact word is not present in the project title.

The tag structure should be normalized rather than storing comma-separated tags in one column.

### Module D — Related/Similar Project Discovery
The system may provide a deterministic "related projects" result.

For example, if a project/search contains:
`theft`, `electricity`, `IoT`

then existing projects can be ranked according to the number of matching stored tags/attributes.

This is NOT AI similarity. It is database-defined matching/ranking.

### Module E — Administration
Provide controlled CRUD operations for authorized users.

### Module F — Reports / Database Views
Useful reports can include:
- Projects by year
- Projects by domain
- Projects by department
- EDI vs AACP project counts
- Faculty-wise projects
- Most-used technologies
- Most-used tags
- Projects involving a particular domain

These can be implemented using SQL aggregation and views.

### Module G — Performance & Query Optimization
Use a sufficiently large dataset to demonstrate database performance.

Example:
1. Execute a search without the relevant index.
2. Create the appropriate index.
3. Execute the same query.
4. Compare execution time/query plan.

This directly supports the syllabus requirement around indexing and query optimization.

