## 9. DBMS Syllabus Mapping

### Unit 1 — DBMS & Data Models
Application:
- Database architecture
- Users and roles
- Relational data model
- Database-backed application structure

### Unit 2 — ER Modeling & Relational Model
Application:
- ER diagram
- Entities/attributes/relationships
- Cardinalities
- Strong/weak entities where justified
- Generalization/specialization where justified
- Relational schema
- Foreign keys
- Referential integrity
- Relational algebra representations of important queries
- Joins

### Unit 3 — SQL & PL/SQL
Application:
- DDL
- DML
- DCL
- DTL
- Search queries
- Joins
- Set operations
- GROUP BY / HAVING / ORDER BY
- Subqueries
- Views
- Indexes
- Stored procedures
- Functions
- Triggers
- Roles and privileges

Note: the course syllabus labels this unit "SQL and PLSQL"; the exact implementation language/features should follow what the selected DBMS supports and what is expected by the faculty.

### Unit 4 — Dependencies & Normalization
Application:
- Identify functional dependencies
- Analyze possible update/insert/delete anomalies
- Normalize the schema
- Demonstrate 1NF, 2NF, 3NF, BCNF and, where applicable, 4NF
- Explain lossless decomposition and dependency preservation

### Unit 5 — Transactions & Query Optimization
Application:
- Transaction-based project updates
- ACID demonstration
- Concurrency considerations
- Indexing
- Query execution/performance comparison
- Query optimization
- Join strategy discussion

### Unit 6 — NoSQL
This project is fundamentally relational.

A MongoDB component should NOT be forced into the core system merely to claim NoSQL usage.

If required for course coverage, a small comparative/experimental MongoDB component can be created separately to demonstrate:
- Document representation
- CRUD
- Indexing
- Aggregation
- RDBMS vs MongoDB comparison

